<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ganges plain</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gangetic, Ganges</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ganges plain is the fertile lowland of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> drained by the Ganges River. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>, whose agricultural wealth supported large states, and it lay at the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>'s sphere in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ganges-plain.jpg" alt="Ganges plain" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeroen · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indo-Gangetic_Plain.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Politically, the dynasty made the Deccan a lasting counterweight to the kingdoms of the <b>Ganges plain</b> and drew the Kannada country into the mainstream of Indian statecraft.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>King Gangaraja (r. 413–?) abdicated and went on pilgrimage to the <b>Ganges</b>, perhaps the only Southeast Asian ruler known to have travelled to India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ghurids came to control the cities of Herat, Ghazni and Balkh, the trade routes between Khurasan and India and, from the 1190s, the rich farmland of the Punjab and the <b>Ganges plain</b>, whose treasuries and temples yielded enormous booty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gupta Empire arose in the <b>Ganges plain</b> in a period of political fragmentation that followed the decline of the Kushan Empire in the northwest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>For two centuries the dynasty fought what historians call the tripartite struggle for control of Kannauj and the <b>Ganges plain</b> with the Pala Empire of Bengal and the Rashtrakuta dynasty of the Deccan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Magadha lay south of the <b>Ganges</b> in what is now southern Bihar, a land of alluvial plains bounded by the river Son to the west and the forested hills of the Chota Nagpur plateau to the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
